A) What is the repo data-carousel-AM intended to do ?
- It's a small ATLAS data staging analysis workflow that pulls two data sources, enriches dataset names.
- On one side, it retrieves data carousel recall history from ATLAS OpenSearch (the atlas_datacarousel-* indices)
- On another side, it queries the DEFT DB in CERN Oracle for production request metadata
- The intended flow is: fetch logs from OpenSearch, enrich them using the DatasetParsing module, analyze them in a notebook, then merge in DEFT request from Oracle.

A.1) Recent upgrade of the data-carousel-AM repo: 
- it's upgraded to use the new format of the documents in OpenSearch. The new document format contains more information (e.g. task_id, task_group,
dataset size etc) than the previous documents, which allows us to not rely on the DEFT part anymore. We can ignore the DEFT part now.
- A new script @fetch_datacarousel_csv.py is created: check out its comments for more details

A.2) What are in the new csv file (e.g. @data-carousel-AM/data/prodsyslogs.csv)
- source_tape is the name of the tape site that the recall request was sent to, i.e. where the dataset was recalled from
- source_rse is the name of the Rucio Storage Resource (RSE), because some sites put real data and Monte Carlo (MC) data on different RSEs, conventionally ATLAS
tape sites create a XXX_DATATAPE RSE to store real data, and a XXX_MCTAPE RSE to store MC data, e.g. IN2P3-CC_MCTAPE vs IN2P3-CC_DATATAPE (here IN2P3-CC is the
name of the ATLAS Tier-1 site)
- destination_rse is where the recalled dataset is sent to, usually a _DATADISK RSE, ie. disk storage where ATLAS jobs can read directly as inputs
- timeEvent is when the recall request was sent (to Rucio) by Data Carousel in PanDA (UTC)
- dataset is the name of the dataset to recall
- total_files is the total number of files in the dataset
- dataset_size is the total size of the dataset (bytes), usually we convert them to GB (not GiB!)
- task_id is the ID of the task that requests this dataset as input
- task_type refers to whether the task is for managed production or user analysis 
- task_group is the physics group of the task
- task_user is the PanDA user name who triggers creation of the task
- ddm_rule_id is the ID of the Rucio Rule that Rucio creates for the staging request of this dataset
- scope : name of scope of the dataset 
- dataset_format: exact ATLAS dataset data type (AOD, EVNT, DAOD_PHYS, etc)
- dataset_format_short: normalized/grouped version of the format, used to reduce category fragmentation (e.g. all DAOD_* --> DAOD)
- to_pin : =1, true, means to pin the dataset after recalled, =0 means still to be recalled, which is what we need here
- dataset_origin : Monte Carlo (mc) or real data (data)
- runNumber|datasetID : runNumber (for data) or dataset id (for MC)
- physicsShort|StreamName: stream Name (for data) or physicsShort description (for mc)
- production_step : e.g. "merge" for merging small outputs, or "recon" for reconstruction step
- dataset_origin : is this a real dataset (data) or Monte Carlo dataset (mc)
- dataset_category : standard, meaning ? 
- versionTag: the version Tag part of the dataset name nomenclature, composed of amiTag and task id
- amiTag : the ami tag part of the version tag
- tid : is the task id part of the version tag, here the task refers to the panda task that produced this dataset as output in the past
- full_tid : is the complete task id, including the sub-task sequential numbers 
- method: internal function call of the panda JEDI that corresponds to this OpenSearch entry, "stage_request" means sending the staging request,
  which is exactly the time stamp we want to collect when studying the recall history

A.3) Between the old and new format of the OpenSearch data carousel records, there is the following mappings of the column names:
    old vs new
- resource vs source_tape
- files vs total_files
- asctime vs timeEvent
- datamc vs dataset_origin
- dataType vs dataset_format_short
- productionStep vs production_step

Other columns not mentioned in the above list are either with the same name or only available in one csv file

B) What is the arcmeta repo for ?
- this repo is to analyze the recall history csv file that is produced by the data-carousel-AM package.
- read through the @arcmeta/README.md file to understand what scripts are available to use in this repo.



